    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2412.14219_rag::chunk_016</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2506.12417_rag::chunk_005</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2503.04398_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2505.11432_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2504.19925_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2407.04656_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">1qP3lsatCR_rag::chunk_004</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2407.04656_rag::chunk_010</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2509.09420_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2505.08944_rag::chunk_001</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2411.15419_rag::chunk_005</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">339caf45a6fa281cae8adc6465343464-Paper-Conference_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2510.02613_rag::chunk_004</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2401.08383_rag::chunk_012</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2408.04307_rag::chunk_004</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Expert parallelism is a key technique for deploying large MoE models across multiple devices to enable distributed execution.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Expert parallelism ( EP ) [ 12 ] combines aspects of data parallelism and model parallelism to support MoE s.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">EP partitions and computes experts in parallel across GPUs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">despite the cross-node expert parallelism common in existing systems</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To avoid the memory overhead of allocating a copy of every expert on every rank, systems use expert parallelism [ 11 , 23 , 51 ]. As illustrated in Figure 1 , expert parallelism distributes experts across ranks (i.e., GPUs).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In expert parallel training, the experts of each layer are split into equal-sized chunks and allocated across multiple GPUs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Since the experts are distributed across different devices, communication during this process i</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In traditional expert parallelism, each token can be simply dispatched to the GPU that owns the corresponding expert</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">cluster deployments favor Expert Parallelism (EP) to reduce inter-node communication</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">With EP , the self-attention and router layers are replicated across GPUs (data parallelism), but each GPU only loads a subset of exp</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">shard experts across GPUs to fit large MoE models (expert parallelism).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Expert parallelism is a key technique for deploying large MoE models across multiple devices to enable distributed execution. As Figure 7 -(a) shown, when distributing MoE layers, each device holds a subset of experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">expert parallelism [6], [5] has been proposed as a novel hybrid parallelism dedicated to MoE by exploiting its unique features</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Expert-parallelism Once the gating function determines expert assignments</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">expert parallelism distributes experts across ranks (i.e., GPUs). Each rank hosts a fixed number of expert slots</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">expert parallelism (EP) [ 17 ] has been proposed and has become the de facto approach to train large-scale MoE models.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Expert parallelism (EP) distributes experts across devices, with tokens dynamically routed to their assigned experts.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In the vanilla expert parallelism, each MoE layer strictly requires two Alltoall collectives</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">cluster deployments favor Expert Parallelism (EP) to reduce inter-node communication, often replicating hot experts to improve load balance</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a notable distinction between EP and other parallelism is EPs flexibility in distributing diverse parameters across DP ranks.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To avoid the memory overhead of allocating a copy of every expert on every rank, systems use expert parallelism</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#language_model">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/S0/R1#model"/>
    <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Language Model</rdfs:label>
    <skos:skos_definition rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A type of model architecture that can serve as a source for experts in an integrated framework.</skos:skos_definition>
    <hasCQ rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CQ_S0_R1_0018</hasCQ>
    <prov:wasDerivedFrom rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">CQ_S0_R1_0018</prov:wasDerivedFrom>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2509.18542_rag::chunk_011</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">all source models share an identical architecture and are all language models. This constraint limits the pool of potential experts</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#Axiom_2">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://example.org/S0/R1#OntologyAxiom"/>
    <axiomType rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">subclass</axiomType>
