    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2502.19811_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Parameters corresponding to each partition is referred to as an expert, and this architecture is known as the Sparse M</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Grouping high-affinity experts on the same device</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the experts are distributed across GPUs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Typically, each expert in the MoE layer is a compact neural network consisting of several feed-forward layers followed by an activation function</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Exp. represents individual experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">expert models that provide diverse but complementary insights into the data distribution</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">when the load exceeds the capacity, experts must discard excess tokens to adhere to the constraint.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">each expert receives 1 /N fraction of tokens</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Figures 5a , 5b , and 5c compare the perplexity achieved by our method and the dense baseline on the routed dataset segments for the 1 . 3 B parameter model, utilizing mixtures of 4 , 16 , and 32 experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">each GPU accommodates more than one expert during inference</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Even modest batch sizes (8-16) cause all experts to be activated</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">layer-wise routers select the l -th layer expert i</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">distributing tokens evenly across experts maximizes computational efficiency</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoE models activate only specific experts per token, unlike dense models that utilize the entire model for every token.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">assigning a gating score for each expert using a trainable linear layer</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">route each input token to a subset of experts for computation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the most burdened experts dictate the overall latency of the MoE layer</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the expert with the highest gating weight for each token is selected to process the input</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">if a token activates an expert not cached in fast memory</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the expert is usually contr</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">parallel FFNs called experts. In each MoE layer, a trainable gate network routes each token to only the top- experts.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">identify the most M relevant experts for the unlearning task</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">allowing each expert to learn independently as a separate neural network</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">integrate multiple smaller sub-models or experts within a comprehensive ensemble model.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Fiddler puts a subset of expert layers into the GPU memory.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">N i represents the number of tokens assigned to the i -th expert</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">necessitating uniform input batch sizes across all experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">distributes the input data to the selected experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">experts are distributed across different devices</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">tokens are only routed to partial experts for computation.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">consists of N experts, each instantiated as an independent FFN.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The tokens in are then processed by the appropriate experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Compared to Expert Drop, both Layer Drop and Block Drop focus on structures beyond expert level</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">incorporating multiple expert networks and employing a sparse gating mechanism</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">each FP16 expert contains approximately 4 , 096 14 , 336 2 2 bytes of weight data.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Switch Transformer [6] with 256 experts per block requires more than 50GB memory</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">activating only a subset of parameters (experts) for each input.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We move the expert parameters to CPU memory to reduce burden on GPU memory.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">parallel FFNs called experts.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">route each token to a top- k subset of experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoEs learn to route inputs to a sparse set of their feedforward parameters.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">each expert is composed of a different LoRA and origin FFN layer</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoE with 4 or 8 experts shows the best quality (lowest validation loss) under a certain inference cost.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">we denote the i th expert on layer j as E i,j</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Parameters corresponding to each partition is referred to as an expert</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">intermediate hidden dimension in the expert FFN layers</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the number of dropped tokens for the i -th expert</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">we solely optimize the router to preserve the explicit semantic distinction of expert modules.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the router does not receive a gradient update from experts that it does not activate</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">resulting in a consistent pattern of token processing by the same experts throughout training</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Each input token is assigned to one or more experts for computation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">layer-wise routers select the l -th layer expert</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
