    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the quality of the MoE model can be continuously improved by increasing the capacity factor</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">four MoE models: Mixtral-8 7B [16] and Switch-32/64/128 with 32, 64, and 128 experts per layer</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">integrate multiple smaller sub-models or experts within a comprehensive ensemble model.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoE differs from dense models by using multiple specialized sub-networks, where each input activates only a subset of experts</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) based sparsely activated neural networks have been introduced for training larger models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">parallel FFNs called experts. In each MoE layer, a trainable gate network routes each token to only the top- experts. As experts are sparsely activated, MoE enables scaling model parameters</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">parallel FFNs called experts. In each MoE layer, a trainable gate network routes each token to only the top- experts.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Since the experts are distributed across different devices, communication during this process i</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Model SRP MoE A:T S:A</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer [ 58 ], consists of multiple feed-forward networks (FFNs), termed experts, and a trainable</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Given the autoregressive nature of Mixture of Experts (MoE) computations, it is vital to maintain consistency across time steps</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoE Jetpack converts pre-trained dense models into MoE models, enhancing convergence and performance</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">within MoE models, thus enhancing efficiency.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoE models differ in the number of active parametersMoE uses just a fraction of them.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The MoE models dense counterparts are selected to be FLOP-equivalent</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Comparison between Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) and Chain-of-Experts (CoE).</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MoE model inference at scale</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the dense and MoE models use 7.02GB and 21.16GB, respectively</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">expert parameters account for the majority of MoE-based LLMs model size (see Figure 3) and only a small fraction of experts are activated during inference</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">distributing input samples to these scattered experts demands resource-intensive all-to-all communication (Fig. 1 ). High communication volume in all-to-all operations significantly hampers the training speed of MoE models</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The sparsely activated mixture of experts (MoE) model presents a promising alternative to traditional densely activated (dense) models</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
