    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2505.11432_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2412.14219_rag::chunk_016</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2410.12013_rag::chunk_004</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2503.05066_rag::chunk_000</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2508.21706_rag::chunk_003</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceChunk rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">339caf45a6fa281cae8adc6465343464-Paper-Conference_rag::chunk_015</hasEvidenceChunk>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In transformer-based large language models (LLMs), each mixture-of-experts (MoE) layer typically consists of a set of N expert networks</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">each mixture-of-experts (MoE) layer typically consists of a set of N expert networks</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Since MoE layer weights 1 Introduction Mixture of Experts (MoE) models have emerged as a powerful paradigm</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">MegaScale-MoE reduces the communication volume by customizing parallelism strategies for the attention and FFN modules in each MoE layer.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">when distributing MoE layers, each device holds a subset of experts along with all other non-expert parameters</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We can derive the load balancing score by calculating the mean of scores across all l MoE layers</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Mixture of Experts (MoE) is an effective architecture for scaling large language models by leveraging sparse expert activation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The MoE layer replaces the standard FFN with a mixture of experts, allowing the model to dynamically select a subset of experts for each input token.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">replacing every other Transformer blocks feed-forward layer with an MoE layer</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
